# A web server, as a program `folio build` makes can be one, on Windows and Linux: each request a browser (or any
# other program) makes is given to a function of the program's, which says what to send back.
#
#   use "server.folio" as web
#   to answer with request
#       if path of request is "/"
#           return "<h1>Hello from Folio</h1>"
#       end
#       return record with status 404, body "There's nothing at " + path of request
#   end
#   web's serve(8080, answer)
#
# A request is a record: its method ("GET", "POST" and the rest), path ("/notes"), query (a dictionary of what
# came after the ?, decoded), version ("HTTP/1.1"), headers (a dictionary, each name in small letters) and body
# (text; body_bytes has its bytes). The function gives back text, sent as a page if it starts with "<" and as
# plain text if not; a list or a dictionary, sent as JSON; or a record with any of status (200 if it has none),
# body (text or bytes), type (as "text/css"), headers (a dictionary) and file (a file's name, whose bytes are the
# body, its type from its name). A mistake in it is sent as 500, and said to the error output, and the server
# goes on.
#
# Many connections at once (DESIGN §123): the server waits for whichever of them has something to say, reads
# what has come and answers when a whole request is there, so one client that sends a byte at a time, or that
# stops halfway, holds up nobody. A connection is kept open for the requests after the first (HTTP/1.1
# keep-alive), and answers them in the order they came, even when they come together. Each request is given
# `timeout` milliseconds to arrive once it has begun, and a kept connection `idle_timeout` to begin the next;
# one that goes over is answered 408 and closed. An answer the other end takes none of for `timeout` is cut
# off. A request bigger than `most_bytes` is answered 413, headers over 64 KB 431, and one that can't be read
# 400. Only `most_connections` are held at once, and while they are, the
# rest wait in the system's own queue.
#
#   web's set_timeout(30000)          # how long a request has to arrive, once begun, or an answer can wait
#   web's set_idle_timeout(5000)      # how long a kept connection may sit with nothing on it
#   web's set_most_bytes(16777216)    # the biggest request, headers and body together
#   web's set_most_connections(1024)  # how many are held at once (by each worker)
#   web's set_most_requests(1000)     # how many a connection answers before it's closed
#   web's set_keep_alive(no)          # a connection closed after each request, as HTTP/1.0 had it
#   web's set_workers(4)              # answered on 4 workers; 0 for one a core
#   web's serve_everywhere(8080, answer)              # from every network the computer is on
#   web's serve_on_workers(8080, answer, 4)           # on 4 workers at once, each a core of its own
#   web's serve_secure(8443, answer, "cert.pem", "key.pem")   # HTTPS
#   web's stop_serving()              # in a handler: serve returns once what's being sent is sent
#
# serve() answers this computer alone (127.0.0.1); serve_everywhere() every network it's on. stop_serving()
# ends serve() once the requests it's answering are sent. Built with --allow-libraries.
#
# Workers (DESIGN §124): set_workers(4), or serve_on_workers(8080, answer, 4), answers on 4 workers at once,
# each with a core, its own copy of the program's variables and its own connections, so a handler that does a
# lot of work holds up only the connections of its own worker. A handler there changes its worker's copy of a
# variable, which no other worker sees, so what is to be kept and shared goes in a database or a file.
# stop_serving() ends the worker it's called on, and serve() ends once every worker has.
#
# serve_secure(443, answer, "cert.pem", "key.pem") answers with HTTPS instead, through OpenSSL 3, with a
# certificate (and the chain after it) and its key, each in a PEM file; serve_secure_everywhere() as well. Linux
# has OpenSSL; on Windows, OpenSSL's two DLLs go beside the program: libssl-3.dll and libcrypto-3.dll, as Python
# has them in its DLLs folder, or libssl-3-x64.dll and libcrypto-3-x64.dll, as Git for Windows has them in its
# mingw64 folder, and the Win64 OpenSSL installer too.

use the library "ws2_32"
    WSAStartup(a whole number, bytes) gives a whole number
    "socket" as win_socket(a whole number, a whole number, a whole number) gives a pointer
    "bind" as win_bind(a pointer, bytes, a whole number) gives a whole number
    "listen" as win_listen(a pointer, a whole number) gives a whole number
    "accept" as win_accept(a pointer, a pointer, a pointer) gives a pointer
    "recv" as win_recv(a pointer, bytes, a whole number, a whole number) gives a whole number
    "send" as win_send(a pointer, bytes, a whole number, a whole number) gives a whole number
    "ioctlsocket" as win_control(a pointer, a whole number, bytes) gives a whole number
    "setsockopt" as win_option(a pointer, a whole number, a whole number, bytes, a whole number) gives a whole number
    "WSAPoll" as win_poll(bytes, a whole number, a whole number) gives a whole number
    closesocket(a pointer) gives a whole number
end
use the library "c"
    "socket" as unix_socket(a whole number, a whole number, a whole number) gives a whole number
    "setsockopt" as unix_option(a whole number, a whole number, a whole number, bytes, a whole number) gives a whole number
    "bind" as unix_bind(a whole number, bytes, a whole number) gives a whole number
    "listen" as unix_listen(a whole number, a whole number) gives a whole number
    "accept" as unix_accept(a whole number, a pointer, a pointer) gives a whole number
    "recv" as unix_recv(a whole number, bytes, a long whole number, a whole number) gives a long whole number
    "send" as unix_send(a whole number, bytes, a long whole number, a whole number) gives a long whole number
    "ioctl" as unix_control(a whole number, a long whole number, bytes) gives a whole number
    "poll" as unix_poll(bytes, a long whole number, a whole number) gives a whole number
    "close" as unix_close(a whole number) gives a whole number
    "sysconf" as unix_sysconf(a whole number) gives a long whole number
end
use the library "kernel32"
    GetTickCount() gives a whole number
    GetActiveProcessorCount(a whole number) gives a whole number
end
use the library "libssl-3" or "libssl-3-x64" or "ssl"
    TLS_server_method() gives a pointer
    SSL_CTX_new(a pointer) gives a pointer
    SSL_CTX_use_certificate_chain_file(a pointer, text) gives a whole number
    SSL_CTX_use_PrivateKey_file(a pointer, text, a whole number) gives a whole number
    SSL_CTX_free(a pointer)
    SSL_new(a pointer) gives a pointer
    SSL_set_fd(a pointer, a whole number) gives a whole number
    SSL_accept(a pointer) gives a whole number
    SSL_read(a pointer, bytes, a whole number) gives a whole number
    SSL_write(a pointer, bytes, a whole number) gives a whole number
    SSL_get_error(a pointer, a whole number) gives a whole number
    SSL_shutdown(a pointer) gives a whole number
    SSL_free(a pointer)
end

# which system it is: a library every Windows has, which no Linux has
set web_windows to yes
try
    GetTickCount()
if it fails
    set web_windows to no
end
set web_running to no
set web_secure to nothing # OpenSSL's context, while answering with HTTPS
set web_cr to (list of 13) as bytes as text
set web_tab to (list of 9) as bytes as text
set web_line to web_cr + "\n"
set web_most to 16777216 # the most a request can be, in bytes
set web_most_head to 65536 # the most its headers can be
set web_timeout to 30000 # milliseconds for a request to arrive, once it has begun
set web_idle_timeout to 5000 # milliseconds a kept connection may sit with nothing on it
set web_most_connections to 1024
set web_most_requests to 1000 # requests one connection answers before it's closed
set web_keep_alive to yes
set web_workers to -1 # how many workers answer: -1 for the program's own thread alone, 0 for one a core
set web_on_worker to no # yes on a worker, which shares the listener with the others
set web_backlog to 512 # connections the system holds for accept() while the server is busy
set web_round_wait to 50 # milliseconds a round waits for something to happen, so time limits are kept
set web_scratch to nothing # where a read puts what came, made once
set web_statuses to dictionary where 200 is "OK", 201 is "Created", 204 is "No Content", 301 is "Moved Permanently",
    302 is "Found", 303 is "See Other", 304 is "Not Modified", 400 is "Bad Request", 401 is "Unauthorized",
    403 is "Forbidden", 404 is "Not Found", 405 is "Method Not Allowed", 408 is "Request Timeout", 409 is "Conflict",
    413 is "Content Too Large", 431 is "Request Header Fields Too Large",
    500 is "Internal Server Error", 503 is "Service Unavailable"
set web_types to dictionary where "html" is "text/html; charset=utf-8", "htm" is "text/html; charset=utf-8",
    "css" is "text/css; charset=utf-8", "js" is "text/javascript; charset=utf-8", "json" is "application/json",
    "txt" is "text/plain; charset=utf-8", "folio" is "text/plain; charset=utf-8", "png" is "image/png", "jpg" is "image/jpeg", "jpeg" is "image/jpeg",
    "gif" is "image/gif", "svg" is "image/svg+xml", "ico" is "image/x-icon", "wasm" is "application/wasm",
    "pdf" is "application/pdf"

# answers requests to this computer alone, on `port`, with `handler`, until stop_serving()
to serve with port, handler
    web_serve(no, port, handler)
end

# answers requests from every network the computer is on
to serve_everywhere with port, handler
    web_serve(yes, port, handler)
end

# answers on `workers` workers at once, 0 for one a core
to serve_on_workers with port, handler, workers
    set_workers(workers)
    web_serve(no, port, handler)
end

# serve, and the rest, return once what's being sent is sent: called from a handler, or a task
to stop_serving
    set web_running to no
end

# how long a request has to arrive, in milliseconds, once its first byte has come
to set_timeout with milliseconds
    set web_timeout to milliseconds
end

# how long a connection kept for another request may sit with nothing on it, in milliseconds
to set_idle_timeout with milliseconds
    set web_idle_timeout to milliseconds
end

# the biggest request, headers and body together, in bytes
to set_most_bytes with how_many
    set web_most to how_many
end

# how many connections are held at once (by each worker); while they are, the rest wait in the system's queue
to set_most_connections with how_many
    set web_most_connections to how_many
end

# how many requests one connection answers before it's closed
to set_most_requests with how_many
    set web_most_requests to how_many
end

# whether a connection is kept open for the requests after the first
to set_keep_alive with keeping
    set web_keep_alive to keeping
end

# how many workers answer requests at once, each on a core of its own; 0 for one a core
to set_workers with how_many
    if not (how_many is divisible by 1) or how_many is less than 0
        fail with "set_workers needs a whole number of workers from 0 up, not " + how_many
    end
    set web_workers to how_many
end

# with HTTPS: the certificate's PEM file (the chain after it, if it has one) and its key's
to serve_secure with port, handler, certificate, key
    web_serve_secure(no, port, handler, certificate, key)
end

# answers requests with HTTPS, as serve_secure does, from every network the computer is on
to serve_secure_everywhere with port, handler, certificate, key
    web_serve_secure(yes, port, handler, certificate, key)
end

to web_serve_secure with everywhere, port, handler, certificate, key
    set context to nothing
    try
        set context to SSL_CTX_new(TLS_server_method())
    if it fails
        fail with "HTTPS needs OpenSSL 3: on Linux, its libssl; on Windows, libssl-3.dll and libcrypto-3.dll (or the -x64 ones) beside the program, as Python has them in its DLLs folder"
    end
    if SSL_CTX_use_certificate_chain_file(context, certificate) is not 1
        SSL_CTX_free(context)
        fail with "can't use the certificate in " + certificate + ": it isn't there, or isn't one in PEM"
    end
    if SSL_CTX_use_PrivateKey_file(context, key, 1) is not 1
        SSL_CTX_free(context)
        fail with "can't use the key in " + key + ": it isn't there, isn't one in PEM, or isn't the certificate's"
    end
    set web_secure to context
    try
        web_serve(everywhere, port, handler)
    if it fails with why
        set web_secure to nothing
        SSL_CTX_free(context)
        fail with why
    end
    set web_secure to nothing
    SSL_CTX_free(context)
end

to web_serve with everywhere, port, handler
    set listener to web_listener(everywhere, port)
    set host to "127.0.0.1"
    if everywhere
        set host to "0.0.0.0"
    end
    set scheme to "http"
    if web_secure is not nothing
        set scheme to "https"
    end
    set how_many to web_workers
    if how_many is 0
        set how_many to web_cores()
    end
    set on to ""
    if how_many is at least 1
        set on to ", on " + how_many + " workers"
    end
    say "listening on " + scheme + "://" + host + ":" + port + "/" + on to the error output
    if how_many is less than 1
        try
            web_loop(listener, handler)
        if it fails with why
            web_close(listener)
            fail with why
        end
    else
        set web_on_worker to yes # what each worker starts with, as it has a copy of the variables as they are
        set running to empty list
        for each k from 1 to how_many
            add (start web_worker(listener, handler) on another core) to running
        end
        # until every worker has stopped serving: one that ends with a mistake is said, and started again
        repeat while length of running is greater than 0
            set going to empty list
            for each one in running
                if one has ended
                    try
                        wait for one
                    if it fails with why
                        say "a worker stopped with a mistake, so another was started: " + why to the error output
                        add (start web_worker(listener, handler) on another core) to going
                    end
                else
                    add one to going
                end
            end
            set running to going
            if length of running is greater than 0
                wait 200 milliseconds
            end
        end
        set web_on_worker to no
    end
    web_close(listener)
end

# a worker's own rounds, on the listener every worker shares
to web_worker with listener, handler
    web_loop(listener, handler)
    return nothing
end

# how many cores this computer has, or 1 if it can't say
to web_cores
    try
        set n to GetActiveProcessorCount(65535) # every processor group
        if n is at least 1
            return n
        end
    if it fails
    end
    try
        set n to unix_sysconf(84) # _SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN
        if n is at least 1
            return n
        end
    if it fails
    end
    return 1
end

# the rounds, until stop_serving: whichever connections have something to say are seen to, the rest left be
to web_loop with listener, handler
    set web_running to yes
    if web_scratch is nothing
        set web_scratch to 65536 bytes
    end
    web_blocking(listener, no)
    set live to empty dictionary # each connection, by its socket
    repeat while web_running
        web_round(listener, handler, live, no)
    end
    repeat while length of keys of live is greater than 0 # the answers being sent, sent, and nothing more read
        web_round(listener, handler, live, yes)
    end
end

# One round: a wait for anything to happen, then the connections that can go on, and the ones out of time. A
# mistake in one connection's part of it, which would be one of the server's own, closes that one alone.
to web_round with listener, handler, live, draining
    set ones to empty list
    for each one in keys of live
        add item one of live to ones
    end
    set taking to not draining and length of ones is less than web_most_connections
    set came to web_wait(listener, taking, ones, web_round_wait)
    set at to 0
    if taking
        set at to 1
        if item 1 of came is not 0
            web_take_in(listener, live)
        end
    end
    set moment to the clock
    for each k from 1 to length of ones
        set c to item k of ones
        set what_came to item (at + k) of came
        if open of c
            try
                if draining and stage of c is not "writing"
                    web_finish(c, live)
                else if what_came is not 0
                    web_step(c, what_came, handler, live)
                else if moment is at least due of c
                    web_out_of_time(c, live)
                end
            if it fails with why
                say "a mistake in the server, so a connection was closed: " + why to the error output
                web_finish(c, live)
            end
        end
    end
end

# The connections waiting to be accepted, taken in while there's room for them. On a worker, one a round, so
# the other workers, which wake for them too, have their share.
to web_take_in with listener, live
    set room to web_most_connections - length of keys of live
    if web_on_worker and room is greater than 1
        set room to 1
    end
    repeat room times
        set client to web_accept(listener)
        if not web_real(client)
            stop
        end
        web_blocking(client, no)
        web_no_delay(client)
        set c to record with handle client, ssl nothing, stage "reading", buffer nothing, used 0, room 0,
            head_end 0, seen 1, head nothing, wanted -1, chunked no, chunk_at 0, pieces empty list, sized 0,
            trailers no, body nothing, out empty list, out_at 0, due the clock + web_timeout / 1000, keep yes,
            served 0, writing no, closing no, open yes
        if web_secure is not nothing
            set ssl of c to SSL_new(web_secure)
            SSL_set_fd(ssl of c, client)
            set stage of c to "agreeing"
        end
        set item client of live to c
    end
end

# whether accept() gave a connection, or said there is none waiting (-1, which a pointer may show as a number
# far past any socket)
to web_real with handle
    return handle is at least 0 and handle is less than 9007199254740992
end

# a connection that can go on: the handshake, more of its request read, or more of its answer sent
to web_step with c, what_came, handler, live
    if web_went_wrong(what_came)
        web_finish(c, live)
        return nothing
    end
    if stage of c is "agreeing"
        web_agree(c, live)
    else if stage of c is "writing"
        web_push(c, what_came, handler, live)
    else
        web_pull(c, what_came, handler, live)
    end
end

# whether what came says the connection is broken, rather than ready: POLLERR or POLLNVAL
to web_went_wrong with what_came
    if web_windows
        return (what_came bit and 5) is not 0
    end
    return (what_came bit and 40) is not 0
end

# whether what came says the other end has gone (POLLHUP), so a read or write that does nothing never will
to web_hung_up with what_came
    if web_windows
        return (what_came bit and 2) is not 0
    end
    return (what_came bit and 16) is not 0
end

# a connection out of time: 408 if it had begun a request, else just closed
to web_out_of_time with c, live
    if stage of c is "reading" and used of c is greater than 0
        web_refuse(c, live, 408, "The request took too long to arrive")
        return nothing
    end
    web_finish(c, live)
end

# TLS: the browser and the server agree on keys, over as many rounds as it takes
to web_agree with c, live
    set how to SSL_accept(ssl of c)
    if how is 1
        set stage of c to "reading"
        set writing of c to no
        return nothing
    end
    if web_tls_waiting(c, how)
        return nothing
    end
    say "a connection that couldn't agree on TLS with the server" to the error output
    web_finish(c, live)
end

# whether OpenSSL only wants to read or write again, which it says after SSL_accept, SSL_read or SSL_write on
# a socket that doesn't wait; sets which way the connection waits for
to web_tls_waiting with c, how
    set why to SSL_get_error(ssl of c, how)
    if why is 2 # SSL_ERROR_WANT_READ
        set writing of c to no
        return yes
    end
    if why is 3 # SSL_ERROR_WANT_WRITE
        set writing of c to yes
        return yes
    end
    return no
end

# what has come on a connection, read and put with the rest of its request
to web_pull with c, what_came, handler, live
    set got to web_receive(c, web_scratch)
    if got is -2 or (got is -1 and web_hung_up(what_came))
        web_finish(c, live)
        return nothing
    end
    if got is -1 # nothing after all: the next round
        return nothing
    end
    if got is 0 # the other end has said all it will
        set closing of c to yes
        if used of c is 0
            web_finish(c, live)
            return nothing
        end
    else
        if used of c is 0 # the whole of it in one read: its bytes as they are, with nothing copied
            set buffer of c to items 1 to got of web_scratch
            set used of c to got
            set room of c to got
            set due of c to the clock + web_timeout / 1000
        else
            web_add_in(c, web_scratch, got)
        end
        if used of c is greater than web_most
            web_refuse(c, live, 413, "The request is too big")
            return nothing
        end
    end
    web_try_request(c, handler, live)
end

# `got` bytes of `from`, put after what the connection has already
to web_add_in with c, from, got
    if used of c + got is greater than room of c
        set bigger to (2 * (used of c + got)) bytes
        set holding to buffer of c
        for each k from 1 to used of c
            set byte k of bigger to byte k of holding
        end
        set buffer of c to bigger
        set room of c to length of bigger
    end
    set holding to buffer of c
    set at to used of c
    for each k from 1 to got
        set byte (at + k) of holding to byte k of from
    end
    set used of c to at + got
end

# The request a connection has so far, answered if all of it is there. The headers come first, and say how
# long the body is, or that it comes in chunks; `wanted` is then where the request's last byte is.
to web_try_request with c, handler, live
    if head_end of c is 0
        set head_end of c to web_head_end(c)
        if head_end of c is 0
            if used of c is greater than web_most_head
                web_refuse(c, live, 431, "The request's headers are too long")
            else if closing of c
                web_finish(c, live)
            end
            return nothing
        end
        if head_end of c is greater than web_most_head
            web_refuse(c, live, 431, "The request's headers are too long")
            return nothing
        end
        set head of c to web_head((items 1 to (head_end of c - 1) of buffer of c) as text)
        if head of c is nothing
            web_refuse(c, live, 400, "The request can't be read")
            return nothing
        end
        if chunked of head of c
            set chunked of c to yes
            set chunk_at of c to head_end of c + 4
        else
            set said to body_size of head of c
            if said is less than 0
                web_refuse(c, live, 400, "The request's length can't be read")
                return nothing
            end
            set wanted of c to head_end of c + 3 + said
            if wanted of c is greater than web_most
                web_refuse(c, live, 413, "The request is too big")
                return nothing
            end
        end
    end
    if chunked of c and body of c is nothing
        set found to web_chunks(c)
        if found is "more"
            if closing of c
                web_finish(c, live)
            end
            return nothing
        end
        if found is "big"
            web_refuse(c, live, 413, "The request is too big")
            return nothing
        end
        if found is "bad"
            web_refuse(c, live, 400, "The request's chunks can't be read")
            return nothing
        end
    end
    if used of c is less than wanted of c
        if closing of c
            web_finish(c, live)
        end
        return nothing
    end
    web_answer(c, handler, live)
end

# a whole request given to the handler, and what it gave back made ready to send
to web_answer with c, handler, live
    set body_bytes to body of c
    if body_bytes is nothing
        set body_bytes to items (head_end of c + 4) to (wanted of c) of buffer of c
    end
    set request to web_request(head of c, body_bytes)
    set answer to nothing
    try
        set answer to handler(request)
    if it fails with why
        say "a mistake answering " + method of request + " " + path of request + ": " + why to the error output
        set answer to record with status 500, body "A mistake: " + why
    end
    set keep of c to web_keeps(c)
    set served of c to served of c + 1
    web_move_on(c)
    web_ready(c, answer, method of request)
    web_push(c, 0, handler, live)
end

# whether this connection is kept open for another request: the program's say, how many it has answered, what
# the request's own HTTP version and Connection header asked for, and whether the other end has finished
to web_keeps with c
    if not web_keep_alive or closing of c or served of c + 1 is at least web_most_requests
        return no
    end
    set said to ""
    if "connection" is in headers of head of c
        set said to lowercase of item "connection" of headers of head of c
    end
    if position of "close" in said is greater than 0
        return no
    end
    if version of head of c is "HTTP/1.1"
        return yes
    end
    return position of "keep-alive" in said is greater than 0 # HTTP/1.0 keeps one only if it asks
end

# this request's bytes dropped, keeping whatever came after it, which is the next request (sent together)
to web_move_on with c
    set over to wanted of c
    if over is less than 0 or over is greater than used of c
        set over to used of c
    end
    if over is at least used of c
        set used of c to 0
    else
        set buffer of c to items (over + 1) to (used of c) of buffer of c
        set used of c to used of c - over
        set room of c to length of buffer of c
    end
    set head_end of c to 0
    set seen of c to 1
    set head of c to nothing
    set wanted of c to -1
    set chunked of c to no
    set chunk_at of c to 0
    set pieces of c to empty list
    set sized of c to 0
    set trailers of c to no
    set body of c to nothing
end

# a status and some words sent, and the connection closed once they are
to web_refuse with c, live, status, words
    set keep of c to no
    set closing of c to yes
    web_ready(c, (record with status status, body words), "GET")
    web_push(c, 0, nothing, live)
end

# what the handler gave, made the bytes to send: the status line and headers, then the body
to web_ready with c, answer, method
    set status to 200
    set body to ""
    set kind to nothing
    set extra to nothing
    if answer is nothing
        set status to 204
    else if web_is_text(answer)
        set body to answer
    else if web_is_collection(answer)
        set body to answer as json
        set kind to "application/json"
    else
        set status to web_field(answer, "status", 200)
        set body to web_field(answer, "body", "")
        set kind to web_field(answer, "type", nothing)
        set extra to web_field(answer, "headers", nothing)
        set named to web_field(answer, "file", nothing)
        if named is not nothing
            try
                set body to read bytes named
                if kind is nothing
                    set kind to web_type_of(named)
                end
            if it fails
                set status to 404
                set body to "There's no file called '" + named + "'"
            end
        end
    end
    if web_is_collection(body)
        set body to body as json
        if kind is nothing
            set kind to "application/json"
        end
    end
    if not web_answer_sound(status, kind, extra) # a line break in a header would end it there and start another, or
        # another answer, of the client's making if a handler put what it was sent there (DESIGN §188)
        set status to 500
        set body to "This answer had a line break in a header, or a status that isn't a number from 100 to 999, so it wasn't sent."
        set kind to "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
        set extra to nothing
    end
    set sent to body
    if web_is_text(body)
        set sent to body as bytes
        if kind is nothing
            set kind to "text/plain; charset=utf-8"
            if length of body is greater than 0 and item 1 of body is "<"
                set kind to "text/html; charset=utf-8"
            end
        end
    end
    if kind is nothing
        set kind to "application/octet-stream"
    end
    set reason to "OK"
    if status is in web_statuses
        set reason to item status of web_statuses
    end
    set head to "HTTP/1.1 " + status + " " + reason + web_line + "Content-Type: " + kind + web_line
    set head to head + "Content-Length: " + length of sent + web_line
    if keep of c # and how long it's kept, in whole seconds, where that's 1 or more
        set head to head + "Connection: keep-alive" + web_line
        set seconds to (web_idle_timeout / 1000) rounded down
        if seconds is at least 1
            set head to head + "Keep-Alive: timeout=" + seconds + web_line
        end
    else
        set head to head + "Connection: close" + web_line
    end
    if extra is not nothing
        for each name in extra
            set head to head + name + ": " + item name of extra + web_line
        end
    end
    # HEAD asks for the headers alone, and 204 and 304 have no body: sending one would be read as the next answer
    set blocks to list of ((head + web_line) as bytes)
    if length of sent is greater than 0 and method is not "HEAD" and status is not 204 and status is not 304
        if web_is_text(body) # the whole answer in one, which goes as one
            set blocks to list of ((head + web_line + body) as bytes)
        else
            add sent to blocks
        end
    end
    set out of c to blocks
    set out_at of c to 0
    set stage of c to "writing"
    set writing of c to yes
    set due of c to the clock + web_timeout / 1000
end

# As much of the answer sent as the connection will take now, and the rest when it says it will take more
# (what_came); then it's kept for another request, or closed. what_came is 0 when the connection hasn't said.
to web_push with c, what_came, handler, live
    repeat while length of out of c is greater than 0
        set block to item 1 of out of c
        set piece to block
        if out_at of c is greater than 0 or length of block is greater than 262144 # at most 256 KB copied a time
            set last to out_at of c + 262144
            if last is greater than length of block
                set last to length of block
            end
            set piece to items (out_at of c + 1) to last of block
        end
        set sent to web_send_some(c, piece)
        if sent is -2 or (sent is -1 and web_hung_up(what_came))
            web_finish(c, live)
            return nothing
        end
        if sent is -1 # it will take no more just now: the round tells when it will
            return nothing
        end
        set out_at of c to out_at of c + sent
        set due of c to the clock + web_timeout / 1000 # as long again for the rest, as it's taking it
        if out_at of c is at least length of block
            set out of c to items 2 to (length of out of c) of out of c
            set out_at of c to 0
        end
    end
    set writing of c to no
    if not keep of c
        web_finish(c, live)
        return nothing
    end
    set stage of c to "reading"
    set due of c to the clock + web_idle_timeout / 1000
    if used of c is greater than 0 and handler is not nothing # another request came with the one just answered
        set due of c to the clock + web_timeout / 1000
        web_try_request(c, handler, live)
    end
end

to web_listener with everywhere, port
    set address to 16 bytes # sockaddr_in: AF_INET, the port high byte first, and the address
    set byte 1 of address to 2
    set byte 3 of address to (shift port right by 8) bit and 255
    set byte 4 of address to port bit and 255
    if not everywhere
        set byte 5 of address to 127
        set byte 8 of address to 1
    end
    set taken to "can't listen on port " + port + ": something else is using it"
    if web_windows
        WSAStartup(514, 408 bytes)
        set listener to win_socket(2, 1, 6)
        if win_bind(listener, address, 16) is not 0
            closesocket(listener)
            fail with taken
        end
        win_listen(listener, web_backlog)
        return listener
    end
    set listener to unix_socket(2, 1, 0)
    set one to 4 bytes # SO_REUSEADDR: a port just used can be used again at once
    set byte 1 of one to 1
    unix_option(listener, 1, 2, one, 4)
    if unix_bind(listener, address, 16) is not 0
        unix_close(listener)
        fail with taken
    end
    unix_listen(listener, web_backlog)
    return listener
end

to web_accept with listener
    if web_windows
        return win_accept(listener, nothing, nothing)
    end
    return unix_accept(listener, nothing, nothing)
end

# whether a socket waits for what it's asked to do (yes), or says at once that it would have to (no)
to web_blocking with handle, waits
    set value to 4 bytes # an int: 1 for not waiting
    if not waits
        set byte 1 of value to 1
    end
    if web_windows
        win_control(handle, 2147772030, value) # FIONBIO
    else
        unix_control(handle, 21537, value) # FIONBIO
    end
end

# TCP_NODELAY: what's sent goes at once, rather than waiting for the other end to say it has the last of what
# was sent, which it may put off for 40 ms, hoping to say so with an answer of its own
to web_no_delay with handle
    set one to 4 bytes
    set byte 1 of one to 1
    if web_windows
        win_option(handle, 6, 1, one, 4)
    else
        unix_option(handle, 6, 1, one, 4)
    end
end

# Waits up to `waiting` milliseconds for the listener, if `taking`, to have a connection waiting, or for any of
# these connections to be ready to read, or to write for those `writing`; gives what came for each, in their
# order (the listener first, if it's asked about), and 0 for one with nothing.
to web_wait with listener, taking, ones, waiting
    set size to 8 # pollfd: Linux's an int, the events and what came; Windows' the SOCKET, 8 bytes
    set events_at to 5
    set in_bit to 1 # POLLIN
    set out_bit to 4 # POLLOUT
    set came_at to 7 # revents, after the socket and the events asked for
    if web_windows
        set size to 16
        set events_at to 9
        set in_bit to 256 # POLLRDNORM
        set out_bit to 16 # POLLWRNORM
        set came_at to 11
    end
    set n to length of ones
    set first to 0
    if taking
        set first to 1
    end
    if n + first is 0
        return empty list
    end
    set table to ((n + first) * size) bytes
    if taking
        web_put(table, 1, listener)
        set byte events_at of table to in_bit bit and 255
        set byte (events_at + 1) of table to shift in_bit right by 8
    end
    set at to first * size + 1
    for each one in ones
        set want to in_bit
        if writing of one
            set want to out_bit
        end
        web_put(table, at, handle of one)
        set byte (at + events_at - 1) of table to want bit and 255
        set byte (at + events_at) of table to shift want right by 8
        set at to at + size
    end
    set got to 0
    if web_windows
        set got to win_poll(table, n + first, waiting)
    else
        set got to unix_poll(table, n + first, waiting)
    end
    set made to empty list
    set at to came_at
    for each k from 1 to n + first
        if got is at most 0
            add 0 to made
        else
            set low to byte at of table
            set high to byte (at + 1) of table
            add low + 256 * high to made
        end
        set at to at + size
    end
    return made
end

# a socket, from byte `from` of a pollfd: 4 bytes on Linux, 8 on Windows, lowest first
to web_put with b, from, handle
    set left to handle
    set count to 4
    if web_windows
        set count to 8
    end
    for each k from 0 to count - 1
        set byte (from + k) of b to left bit and 255
        set left to shift left right by 8
    end
end

# Up to all of `room`'s bytes of what has come on the connection: how many, 0 once the other end has said all it
# will, -1 if nothing has come after all, or -2 if the connection can't go on.
to web_receive with c, room
    if ssl of c is not nothing
        set got to SSL_read(ssl of c, room, length of room)
        if got is greater than 0
            return got
        end
        if web_tls_waiting(c, got)
            return -1
        end
        if SSL_get_error(ssl of c, got) is 6 # SSL_ERROR_ZERO_RETURN: closed as TLS closes
            return 0
        end
        return -2
    end
    set got to 0
    if web_windows
        set got to win_recv(handle of c, room, length of room, 0)
    else
        set got to unix_recv(handle of c, room, length of room, 0)
    end
    if got is less than 0 # nothing to read just now, or a connection gone, which the next round says
        return -1
    end
    return got
end

# As many of these bytes as the connection will take now: how many, -1 if it will take none just now, or -2 if
# it can't go on.
to web_send_some with c, sent
    if ssl of c is not nothing
        set gone to SSL_write(ssl of c, sent, length of sent)
        if gone is greater than 0
            return gone
        end
        if web_tls_waiting(c, gone)
            return -1
        end
        return -2
    end
    set gone to 0
    if web_windows
        set gone to win_send(handle of c, sent, length of sent, 0)
    else
        set gone to unix_send(handle of c, sent, length of sent, 16384) # MSG_NOSIGNAL: a connection gone is a mistake, not the end
    end
    if gone is less than 1 # its room full, or a connection gone, which the next round says
        return -1
    end
    return gone
end

# a connection done with: closed, and forgotten
to web_finish with c, live
    web_shut(c)
    remove (handle of c) from live
end

to web_shut with c
    if not open of c
        return nothing
    end
    set open of c to no
    if ssl of c is not nothing
        SSL_shutdown(ssl of c)
        SSL_free(ssl of c)
        set ssl of c to nothing
    end
    web_close(handle of c)
end

to web_close with handle
    if web_windows
        closesocket(handle)
    else
        unix_close(handle)
    end
end

# Where the blank line after the headers starts, from 1, or 0 if it hasn't come yet. Each look starts where
# the last one stopped, so headers that come a byte at a time are looked through once.
to web_head_end with c
    set b to buffer of c
    set k to seen of c
    set last to used of c - 3
    repeat while k is at most last
        if byte k of b is 13 and byte (k + 1) of b is 10 and byte (k + 2) of b is 13 and byte (k + 3) of b is 10
            return k
        end
        set k to k + 1
    end
    if k is greater than seen of c
        set seen of c to k
    end
    return 0
end

# The request's first line and headers, read once: its method, target, version and headers (each name in small
# letters), whether its body comes in chunks, and how long it is (-1 if that can't be read); or nothing if the
# first line isn't a request's.
to web_head with words
    set lines to split words by web_line
    set first to split item 1 of lines by " "
    if length of first is not 3 or item 1 of first is "" or item 2 of first is ""
        return nothing
    end
    set headers to empty dictionary
    set said to 0
    set sized to no
    set chunked to no
    set others to items 2 to (length of lines) of lines
    for each one in others
        set colon to position of ":" in one
        if colon is greater than 1
            set name to lowercase of web_trim((items 1 to (colon - 1) of one))
            set value to ""
            if colon is less than length of one
                set value to web_trim((items (colon + 1) to (length of one) of one))
            end
            if name is "content-length"
                set given to web_whole(value)
                if given is less than 0 or (sized and given is not said) # two that differ can't both be right
                    set said to -1
                else if said is not -1
                    set said to given
                end
                set sized to yes
            else if name is "transfer-encoding" and position of "chunked" in lowercase of value is greater than 0
                set chunked to yes
            end
            set item name of headers to value
        else if one is not ""
            return nothing
        end
    end
    return record with method item 1 of first, target item 2 of first, version item 3 of first, headers headers,
        chunked chunked, body_size said
end

# the whole number these digits are, or -1 if they aren't digits
to web_whole with digits
    if digits is "" or length of digits is greater than 15
        return -1
    end
    for each d in digits
        if position of d in "0123456789" is 0
            return -1
        end
    end
    return number of digits
end

# More of a body in chunks read, from where the last look got to: chunk_at is where the next chunk's size line
# starts (or, after the last chunk, the next trailer's), pieces where each chunk's bytes are and how many, in
# turn, and sized how many in all. Gives "more" until it has all come, "big", "bad", or "done", with the body
# made and `wanted` its last byte. Each chunk is its size in hex (and anything after a ;), a line end, its bytes,
# a line end; the last has size 0, then lines of trailers, which are passed over, until an empty one.
to web_chunks with c
    set b to buffer of c
    set used to used of c
    repeat while yes
        set at to chunk_at of c
        set line_end to web_line_end(b, at, used)
        if line_end is 0
            if used - at is greater than web_most_head
                return "bad"
            end
            return "more"
        end
        if trailers of c
            if line_end is at
                set wanted of c to at + 1
                set body of c to web_pieces(b, pieces of c, sized of c)
                return "done"
            end
            set chunk_at of c to line_end + 2
        else
            set size to 0
            set digits to 0
            set k to at
            repeat while k is less than line_end and byte k of b is not 59 # up to a ;
                set d to byte k of b
                if (d is at least 48 and d is at most 57) or (d is at least 65 and d is at most 70) or (d is at least 97 and d is at most 102)
                    set size to size * 16 + web_hex(d)
                    set digits to digits + 1
                else if d is not 32 and d is not 9
                    return "bad"
                end
                if size is greater than web_most
                    return "big"
                end
                set k to k + 1
            end
            if digits is 0
                return "bad"
            end
            if size is 0
                set trailers of c to yes
                set chunk_at of c to line_end + 2
            else
                set begins to line_end + 2
                if begins + size + 1 is greater than used
                    return "more"
                end
                if byte (begins + size) of b is not 13 or byte (begins + size + 1) of b is not 10
                    return "bad"
                end
                set held to pieces of c
                add begins to held
                add size to held
                set sized of c to sized of c + size
                if sized of c is greater than web_most
                    return "big"
                end
                set chunk_at of c to begins + size + 2
            end
        end
    end
end

# the bytes of the chunks, one after another
to web_pieces with b, pieces, how_many
    if length of pieces is 0
        return 0 bytes
    end
    if length of pieces is 2 # one chunk: its bytes as they are
        set begins to item 1 of pieces
        set last to begins + (item 2 of pieces) - 1
        return items (begins) to (last) of b
    end
    set made to how_many bytes
    set at to 0
    for each p from 1 to length of pieces / 2
        set begins to (item (2 * p - 1) of pieces) - 1
        set size to item (2 * p) of pieces
        for each k from 1 to size
            set byte (at + k) of made to byte (begins + k) of b
        end
        set at to at + size
    end
    return made
end

# where the next \r\n is, from byte `from`, or 0 if it hasn't come yet
to web_line_end with b, from, used
    set k to from
    repeat while k is less than used
        if byte k of b is 13 and byte (k + 1) of b is 10
            return k
        end
        set k to k + 1
    end
    return 0
end

# the request the handler is given, from its head and the body's bytes
to web_request with head, body
    set target to target of head
    set path to target
    set query to empty dictionary
    set mark to position of "?" in target
    if mark is greater than 0
        set path to web_text_part(target, 1, mark - 1)
        set query to web_form(web_text_part(target, mark + 1, length of target - mark))
    end
    return record with method method of head, path web_decode(path), query query, version version of head,
        headers headers of head, body body as text, body_bytes body
end

# `count` characters of a text, from character `from`; as many as there are, if it has fewer
to web_text_part with words, from, count
    set last to from + count - 1
    if last is greater than length of words
        set last to length of words
    end
    if from is less than 1 or last is less than from
        return ""
    end
    return items (from) to (last) of words
end

# without the spaces and tabs at either end
to web_trim with words
    set n to length of words
    set first to 1
    repeat while first is at most n and (item first of words is " " or item first of words is web_tab)
        set first to first + 1
    end
    set last to n
    repeat while last is at least first and (item last of words is " " or item last of words is web_tab)
        set last to last - 1
    end
    if last is less than first
        return ""
    end
    return items first to last of words
end

# a=1&b=two%20words, as a dictionary
to web_form with words
    set made to empty dictionary
    for each pair in split words by "&"
        if pair is not ""
            set mark to position of "=" in pair
            if mark is 0
                set item web_decode(pair) of made to ""
            else
                set name to web_decode(web_text_part(pair, 1, mark - 1))
                set item name of made to web_decode(web_text_part(pair, mark + 1, length of pair - mark))
            end
        end
    end
    return made
end

# %20 and + as what they stand for
to web_decode with words
    if position of "%" in words is 0 and position of "+" in words is 0
        return words
    end
    set codes to words as bytes
    set made to empty list
    set k to 1
    repeat while k is at most length of codes
        set b to byte k of codes
        if b is 37 and k + 2 is at most length of codes and web_is_hex(byte (k + 1) of codes) and web_is_hex(byte (k + 2) of codes) # %
            add web_hex(byte (k + 1) of codes) * 16 + web_hex(byte (k + 2) of codes) to made
            set k to k + 3
        else
            if b is 43 # +
                add 32 to made
            else
                add b to made
            end
            add 1 to k
        end
    end
    return made as bytes as text
end

# whether a character's code is a hex digit's
to web_is_hex with code
    return (code is at least 48 and code is at most 57) or (code is at least 65 and code is at most 70) or (code is at least 97 and code is at most 102)
end

# a hex digit's value, from its character's code
to web_hex with code
    if code is at least 97
        return code - 87
    else if code is at least 65
        return code - 55
    end
    return code - 48
end

# a record's field, or `otherwise` if it has none
# whether an answer's status is a whole number from 100 to 999, and its type and each header's name and value have no
# line break (a new line or a carriage return) in them
to web_answer_sound with status, kind, extra
    if not (status is a number) or not (status is divisible by 1) or status is less than 100 or status is greater than 999
        return no
    end
    set parts to empty list
    if kind is not nothing
        add "" + kind to parts
    end
    if extra is not nothing
        for each name in extra
            add "" + name to parts
            add "" + item name of extra to parts
        end
    end
    for each part in parts
        if "\n" is in part or web_cr is in part
            return no
        end
    end
    return yes
end

to web_field with answer, name, otherwise
    try
        if name is "status"
            return status of answer
        else if name is "body"
            return body of answer
        else if name is "type"
            return type of answer
        else if name is "headers"
            return headers of answer
        end
        return file of answer
    if it fails
        return otherwise
    end
end

to web_text with value as text
    return yes
end

to web_list with value as a list
    return yes
end

to web_dictionary with value as a dictionary
    return yes
end

to web_is_text with value
    try
        return web_text(value)
    if it fails
        return no
    end
end

to web_is_collection with value
    try
        return web_list(value)
    if it fails
        try
            return web_dictionary(value)
        if it fails
            return no
        end
    end
end

# a file's type, from what its name ends with
to web_type_of with named
    set parts to split lowercase of named by "."
    set ending to item (length of parts) of parts
    if ending is in web_types
        return item ending of web_types
    end
    return "application/octet-stream"
end
